1
00:00:00,460 --> 00:00:10,520
Hello everyone! Today I'll

2
00:00:11,220 --> 00:00:18,820
be discussing a topic that you must have seen in other modules,

3
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:21,680
especially data structures and maybe even software engineering.

4
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:26,460
Before I go into that topic, let me just remind you of where we are.

5
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:34,540
In the first 5 weeks, we studied the foundations of Isabelle,

6
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:41,240
what is Isabelle, what is theorem proving, what is the syntax, what is the basic interaction mode,

7
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:44,800
how can you interact with it and use it to prove theorems and so on.

8
00:00:44,830 --> 00:00:51,580
In week 6, we worked on proving correctness of algorithms,

9
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:57,980
specified dysfunctional programs, like for example sorting, in particular we studied computation induction and also we studied how

10
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:00,040
to reason about running times and functions.

11
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:08,720
And lastly, in week 7, we focused on how to formally verify implementations of data structures,

12
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:18,060
in particular balanced search trees, and we focused on 2-3 trees, so we focused on how to implement them and how to prove them correct

13
00:01:18,060 --> 00:01:19,760
and to show that they are balanced.

14
00:01:21,590 --> 00:01:25,170
Today we'll be talking about something else, which is called abstract data types.

15
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:37,640
So today's lecture will be about 4 subtopics.

16
00:01:38,120 --> 00:01:41,360
The first one is what is an abstract data type, so I'll just introduce you to the notion.

17
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:46,580
Secondly, I'll give you an example data type, an abstract data type.

18
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:53,960
And thirdly, I'll be talking to you about how to use these abstract data types by showing you an example of how to use abstract data types.

19
00:01:55,800 --> 00:02:03,420
And lastly, I will show you how we can implement these data types to get efficient algorithms out of them.

20
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:10,260
So let's go for the first subtopic.

21
00:02:10,610 --> 00:02:20,130
So if you remember, in weeks 4 and 7, we talked about 2 types of trees.

22
00:02:20,710 --> 00:02:28,430
First was just binary search trees, so we did binary search trees,

23
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:35,930
and here we did balanced search trees.

24
00:02:40,690 --> 00:02:46,330
I'll just call these binary search trees.

25
00:02:50,250 --> 00:02:59,620
So if you remember, the search trees were ones where we knew that there is a certain ordering of

26
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:02,800
the elements.

27
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:12,090
For example, if an element is at the root, we know that it is strictly bigger than all the elements in the left subtree,

28
00:03:12,170 --> 00:03:17,900
and it's strictly smaller than all the elements in the right subtree.

29
00:03:19,380 --> 00:03:25,500
So this was a search tree, and had the advantage that if you were searching,

30
00:03:25,620 --> 00:03:27,160
you know in which direction to go.

31
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:33,440
So if you're searching for something smaller than the root, you go there, otherwise you go to the other subtree.

32
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:42,740
And the second type, which is called balanced, if we use balanced search trees,

33
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:48,020
what we're talking about is making sure that the height of the tree is small enough,

34
00:03:48,220 --> 00:03:58,310
so that whenever you're doing search, the running time of the search is limited due

35
00:03:58,310 --> 00:04:00,130
to the limits on the height,

36
00:04:00,130 --> 00:04:08,330
because you don't want all the tree to be stored in one subtree,

37
00:04:08,430 --> 00:04:11,090
like you don't want all the elements to be here, and then this one to be empty,

38
00:04:11,570 --> 00:04:16,010
which would lead to a very skewed search time.

39
00:04:16,150 --> 00:04:19,270
If you were searching for something smaller than the root, then you would spend a lot of time,

40
00:04:19,330 --> 00:04:21,250
otherwise you'd spend almost no time.

41
00:04:21,250 --> 00:04:30,890
You don't want that, you want balancing, so that the worst case search running time is bounded by the log of the size of the tree.

42
00:04:31,770 --> 00:04:33,410
So that was last week.

43
00:04:43,660 --> 00:04:47,180
Those two trees are ways to implement sets.

44
00:04:48,140 --> 00:04:55,840
You want to implement operations like empty, what is an empty set, which is just an empty tree in the case of the implementation, so it's a leaf,

45
00:04:57,190 --> 00:05:02,650
and what is insert, because we had a function to insert in a tree, and is it, and we have the function to search in a tree.

46
00:05:02,650 --> 00:05:08,790
So now we know that those two trees that we did in those two weeks are just two implementation of sets,

47
00:05:09,250 --> 00:05:14,020
but we actually want two things.

48
00:05:14,220 --> 00:05:23,110
We want the implementation of a set to be something separate, and we want the properties of the set implementation to be something else.

49
00:05:23,110 --> 00:05:24,130
So you have two things.

50
00:05:25,630 --> 00:05:32,090
You have an implementation, which is done with those trees,

51
00:05:32,650 --> 00:05:35,950
so that the implementation is our tree, so this and that are implementations.

52
00:05:36,810 --> 00:05:44,150
And the other thing that you want is properties of all implementations of trees,

53
00:05:44,190 --> 00:05:46,030
that don't depend on the actual implementation,

54
00:05:46,130 --> 00:05:48,530
it's just the properties that have to hold for all of them.

55
00:05:48,530 --> 00:05:52,190
So what are the properties that have to hold for all the implementation of trees?

56
00:05:52,710 --> 00:05:58,730
They all have to have the same interface, and they all have to have the same so-called specification. What does that mean?

57
00:06:00,450 --> 00:06:06,510
A specification is the logical properties, like for example, is in,

58
00:06:07,630 --> 00:06:14,920
always finds an element, even only if it were in the tree, or if it were in the set, because this is an implementation of a set.

59
00:06:15,420 --> 00:06:16,940
That's a part of the specification.

60
00:06:16,940 --> 00:06:26,300
The interface, on the other hand, is what operations does that implementation give you.

61
00:06:26,420 --> 00:06:35,660
So in our case, in the case for sets, and the way we implemented them as trees in the last two weeks,

62
00:06:36,260 --> 00:06:39,500
in weeks 4 and 7, we had three parts of the interface.

63
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:42,700
We had an empty, insert, and isin.

64
00:06:42,700 --> 00:06:48,500
So any implementation of trees has to have those three elements in its interface.

65
00:06:48,580 --> 00:06:55,640
It has to give you a function to build an empty tree, and a function to insert in a tree,

66
00:06:55,640 --> 00:07:01,140
and a function that gives you true or false based on whether a certain element that you're looking for isin't a tree.

67
00:07:02,580 --> 00:07:12,490
Now, any data structure that meets that specification

68
00:07:12,490 --> 00:07:21,730
of an abstract data type, which is namely an interface and a specification, is an implementation of that abstract data type.

69
00:07:22,340 --> 00:07:29,700
So the two trees that we talked about are both implementations of the abstract data type of sets,

70
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:41,780
which means that they both have satisfied interface constraints, so they both have the same functions for a set abstract data type,

71
00:07:41,780 --> 00:07:47,920
and they both satisfy the logical properties of an implementation of a set data type,

72
00:07:47,940 --> 00:07:49,180
namely in the specification.

73
00:07:51,110 --> 00:08:01,580
So, to summarize, an abstract data type, at a very high level, is a way for us to understand

74
00:08:01,580 --> 00:08:06,120
and state the properties of an implementation of a data structure

75
00:08:06,120 --> 00:08:09,060
without actually knowing anything about the implementation of the data structure.

76
00:08:09,780 --> 00:08:16,800
So here, if you want to know the properties of the implementation of a data structure,

77
00:08:16,940 --> 00:08:18,160
you have two types of properties.

78
00:08:18,160 --> 00:08:22,140
One is called the interface, so what functions does it provide you, and the other one is called the specification,

79
00:08:22,240 --> 00:08:30,400
which is what are the logical properties that that implementation satisfies.

80
00:08:32,340 --> 00:08:39,340
So an example abstract data type is the one for sets.

81
00:08:39,980 --> 00:08:43,360
So as I said, we have already implemented sets in rig 4 and 7 using trees,

82
00:08:44,580 --> 00:08:49,470
but if you want to not really care about the actual implementation,

83
00:08:49,610 --> 00:08:57,010
you want to just know the abstract data type of sets, namely what are the properties that any implementation has to satisfy, you have two things, as I said.

84
00:08:57,150 --> 00:09:02,440
You have the interface, the first one, and the second one, we'll come to later, is the specification.

85
00:09:02,680 --> 00:09:05,320
So what is the interface of the set abstract data type?

86
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:12,060
As I said, the interface says what are the functions, so the first function is empty,

87
00:09:12,060 --> 00:09:14,440
the second function is a function that does insertion,

88
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:20,860
the third function is a function that asks whether an element is in the set or not.

89
00:09:22,470 --> 00:09:26,410
And I want you to look here at the types of the three functions,

90
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:31,700
and to pause for a second and think about those types.

91
00:09:31,700 --> 00:09:40,730
So here we're talking about three functions that

92
00:09:40,730 --> 00:09:44,250
are taken as an argument, 's.

93
00:09:45,910 --> 00:09:46,770
So what is 's?

94
00:09:47,210 --> 00:09:51,450
's is actually a polymorphic type, as I mentioned in the first few weeks.

95
00:09:51,890 --> 00:09:59,170
You can talk about a polymorphic type or a type variable that you can instantiate with implementations or concrete values for that type.

96
00:09:59,170 --> 00:10:09,440
So in this case, we have this type 's, and that means, or that is the type, the abstract,

97
00:10:10,360 --> 00:10:15,460
or the polymorphic type that we use to represent a set, an implementation of a set.

98
00:10:15,960 --> 00:10:22,800
So for every abstract data type, we have such a type of interest,

99
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:32,040
only one type of interest, which is the type corresponding to what that abstract data type is supposed to be

100
00:10:32,040 --> 00:10:33,380
implementing.

101
00:10:33,900 --> 00:10:43,970
So here the type, as I said, is s, and our abstract data type is supposed to be implementing a set of type s.

102
00:10:45,330 --> 00:10:54,560
Now, the function empty doesn't take anything as an argument and only returns

103
00:10:54,560 --> 00:10:59,360
to you an empty set of type s.

104
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:05,440
The function insert takes an element of type alpha or a,

105
00:11:09,220 --> 00:11:14,520
and takes a set and returns to you another set

106
00:11:14,520 --> 00:11:21,220
where that other set that you returned is the same as this one, but with that element added to it.

107
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:24,990
So this is the function insert.

108
00:11:25,630 --> 00:11:32,770
And lastly, we have the function isin that takes a set as a first input,

109
00:11:35,670 --> 00:11:42,290
and an alpha as an element to look for, and returns to you a boolean value, so true or false,

110
00:11:42,890 --> 00:11:47,670
based on whether this alpha was in the set that we're talking about or no.

111
00:11:49,150 --> 00:11:55,280
So an abstract data type for a set within this module, within this lecture,

112
00:11:55,280 --> 00:12:02,560
I want you to think about it as only something that will have the interface with three functions.

113
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:07,120
Empty, insert, isin. We're not talking about deletion, we're not talking about unions or any other operations in sets.

114
00:12:08,960 --> 00:12:13,480
So this is the interface of an abstract data type, in our case for sets.

115
00:12:14,160 --> 00:12:20,480
So to summarize that, again, it includes functions, so empty, insert, and isin,

116
00:12:20,480 --> 00:12:27,900
and includes a type of interest, which is, in our case here, is this s,

117
00:12:28,480 --> 00:12:32,440
which basically stands for a set.

118
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:41,900
The next part of an abstract data type is the specification.

119
00:12:41,900 --> 00:12:51,370
So this is a specification of the logical properties of

120
00:12:52,200 --> 00:12:56,100
the functions that implement an abstract data type.

121
00:12:56,820 --> 00:12:57,940
So what does that mean?

122
00:12:59,510 --> 00:13:06,490
So if you remember, I said we have this type T of interest for every abstract data type,

123
00:13:06,490 --> 00:13:09,230
so that can be s, for instance, for a set.

124
00:13:10,210 --> 00:13:19,520
And we want to say that the functions that we have defined on that type s,

125
00:13:19,780 --> 00:13:23,350
like, for example, the function empty or insert or isin,

126
00:13:23,880 --> 00:13:28,020
we want to say that either one of those three functions behaves a certain way.

127
00:13:28,540 --> 00:13:31,020
So how can we say that the function behaves a certain way?

128
00:13:31,020 --> 00:13:38,940
We talk about its input and output, and the relationship between its input and output,

129
00:13:38,940 --> 00:13:42,000
in a mathematical way.

130
00:13:42,140 --> 00:13:44,020
And how do we talk about them in a mathematical way?

131
00:13:44,420 --> 00:13:48,940
We relate that abstract type s, the type of interest that we talked about,

132
00:13:49,040 --> 00:13:57,440
to an existing type in Isabelle, so a type in Isabelle Hall.

133
00:13:57,440 --> 00:14:09,450
So for example, that type could be alpha sets, or it could be alpha lists, etc.

134
00:14:09,570 --> 00:14:13,910
But in our case, in the case of an abstract data type for sets,

135
00:14:14,110 --> 00:14:18,890
we're going to be talking about alpha sets as the mathematical model for the type s.

136
00:14:19,910 --> 00:14:23,890
So that mathematical model allows you to talk about,

137
00:14:23,890 --> 00:14:32,640
to devise a specification for how the functions that the abstract data type provides behave,

138
00:14:32,680 --> 00:14:33,780
for how they behave.

139
00:14:37,100 --> 00:14:39,760
So let's try and make that a little more concrete.

140
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,160
So in order for us to say, for instance,

141
00:14:43,740 --> 00:14:48,360
so what do we want to say in a specification before I go there?

142
00:14:48,360 --> 00:14:54,410
So you want to say that, basically, informally, you want to say that empty,

143
00:14:57,230 --> 00:15:00,130
empty, the function that we provided here, so this function,

144
00:15:00,690 --> 00:15:10,500
you want to say empty, for instance, behaves like the empty set from Isabelle.

145
00:15:10,500 --> 00:15:16,230
So like this, curly brackets, like that.

146
00:15:16,590 --> 00:15:20,710
So how can we say empty always behaves like that?

147
00:15:20,900 --> 00:15:27,810
So first of all, in order for us to say that empty behaves like that,

148
00:15:28,150 --> 00:15:36,880
or that, for example, we also would like to say that is in,

149
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:50,020
which is the function that we provided, behaves like set membership. So how can we say that?

150
00:15:51,120 --> 00:15:53,800
And in general, you want to say these kinds of properties

151
00:15:53,800 --> 00:15:58,500
for whatever your abstract data type provides,

152
00:15:58,620 --> 00:16:00,560
so the function that your abstract data type provides,

153
00:16:00,640 --> 00:16:03,700
you want to relate them all to the existing functions

154
00:16:05,080 --> 00:16:08,780
on a mathematical type that is defined in Isabelle.

155
00:16:08,820 --> 00:16:10,460
For example, in our case here is sets.

156
00:16:11,460 --> 00:16:15,560
So the first way, the main way you can connect these functions,

157
00:16:15,900 --> 00:16:17,820
the functions empty that you provide, for example,

158
00:16:17,920 --> 00:16:19,520
is in to the functions that exist already,

159
00:16:19,520 --> 00:16:25,240
so this empty set symbol there, or this set membership.

160
00:16:25,940 --> 00:16:28,920
The way you can do that is firstly you need to provide a connection

161
00:16:29,520 --> 00:16:33,220
between the two types AT of interest and A,

162
00:16:33,760 --> 00:16:35,540
which is the mathematical type in whole.

163
00:16:35,660 --> 00:16:38,400
So you want to provide a connection, a function alpha,

164
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:47,520
that takes an set S of our type and returns to

165
00:16:47,520 --> 00:16:50,060
you an A set.

166
00:16:51,360 --> 00:16:55,160
So first of all, if you want to connect the functions

167
00:16:55,160 --> 00:16:58,760
that the data type defines to the functions that already exist in Isabelle,

168
00:16:58,760 --> 00:17:01,600
you need to connect firstly the type of tree S

169
00:17:01,860 --> 00:17:06,240
to the mathematical type alpha set in Isabelle with a function.

170
00:17:06,360 --> 00:17:07,980
It's called an abstraction function.

171
00:17:10,300 --> 00:17:14,800
The second thing that I want you to believe for now,

172
00:17:15,180 --> 00:17:17,460
and then we will see how later on,

173
00:17:17,760 --> 00:17:26,940
is that this function alpha takes any S

174
00:17:26,940 --> 00:17:29,980
and gives you a corresponding mathematical alpha set.

175
00:17:30,680 --> 00:17:36,560
But not all the S's, not all the elements in this type,

176
00:17:36,560 --> 00:17:42,740
so not every S will be corresponding to an alpha set.

177
00:17:42,920 --> 00:17:45,800
Not all of them, it's not always the case that they will be like that,

178
00:17:45,820 --> 00:17:47,780
and I will show you why later on.

179
00:17:47,780 --> 00:17:50,700
But I want you to just believe that and keep that in your notes

180
00:17:50,700 --> 00:17:54,080
that not all of them are going to be corresponding,

181
00:17:54,200 --> 00:17:56,260
not all S's are going to be corresponding to alpha sets,

182
00:17:56,980 --> 00:17:58,580
and some of them only will.

183
00:17:58,920 --> 00:18:03,920
So we have an invariant which is a predicate

184
00:18:04,600 --> 00:18:08,080
that takes the type T of interest,

185
00:18:08,240 --> 00:18:11,740
so it takes, in our case, S, in the case of sets, and returns a bool,

186
00:18:11,900 --> 00:18:14,240
and this invariant tells you,

187
00:18:14,280 --> 00:18:18,680
is that set S, does it really correspond,

188
00:18:18,920 --> 00:18:22,200
can you really map it to an alpha set or no?

189
00:18:22,720 --> 00:18:27,060
And if that's true, that means that the mapping

190
00:18:27,060 --> 00:18:31,380
by the function alpha, the abstraction function,

191
00:18:31,380 --> 00:18:33,880
is meaningful.

192
00:18:34,360 --> 00:18:38,280
If the predicate invar is false for a given S,

193
00:18:38,700 --> 00:18:45,360
then we cannot really talk about the abstraction

194
00:18:45,360 --> 00:18:48,080
of S into an alpha set,

195
00:18:48,080 --> 00:18:51,840
because the invariant doesn't hold for S.

196
00:18:53,640 --> 00:18:57,660
So what is this alpha and invar?

197
00:18:57,940 --> 00:18:59,880
There are actually two parts of the interface

198
00:18:59,880 --> 00:19:01,160
that we will always have.

199
00:19:01,160 --> 00:19:05,480
As I said, the interface will have some operations,

200
00:19:05,680 --> 00:19:07,920
like empty, insert, isin,

201
00:19:08,420 --> 00:19:10,460
and then, in addition to that,

202
00:19:10,520 --> 00:19:12,640
you also will have two more parts of the interface.

203
00:19:13,080 --> 00:19:15,240
Firstly, the abstraction function alpha,

204
00:19:15,840 --> 00:19:19,590
and secondly, the invariant predicate invar,

205
00:19:19,650 --> 00:19:24,550
which identifies which elements of the abstract type T

206
00:19:24,550 --> 00:19:31,170
are the ones that correspond to an element of the mathematical type,

207
00:19:31,170 --> 00:19:34,010
like for example here alpha set in our case.

208
00:19:36,330 --> 00:19:38,750
And those functions are only, as I said,

209
00:19:39,250 --> 00:19:43,150
useful for specification and verification purposes.

210
00:19:43,270 --> 00:19:45,650
They don't serve any implementation,

211
00:19:46,470 --> 00:19:48,070
and actual implementation uses the basic.

212
00:19:48,610 --> 00:19:51,110
Basically, the reason we have an alpha

213
00:19:51,110 --> 00:19:55,270
is to be able to say things like empty behaves like this,

214
00:19:55,490 --> 00:19:58,550
like empty here, and isin, the function isin,

215
00:19:58,750 --> 00:20:01,430
behaves like the set membership function

216
00:20:02,870 --> 00:20:04,890
and the only way for you to connect these functions

217
00:20:04,890 --> 00:20:07,410
is first to connect the things that they take as input

218
00:20:07,410 --> 00:20:08,870
and the things that they return as output,

219
00:20:08,870 --> 00:20:10,250
and the way to do that connection,

220
00:20:10,410 --> 00:20:14,010
to make that connection, is using the function, alpha, the abstraction function.

